2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The task is to find an increasing alphabet block p1<...<p10, 1≤pi≤26, with ri = digit-sum(pi) reduced to one digit, and ri equals the given list 6,7,?,3,5,7,8,9,1,8.

Digital-root constraints give
r1=6 → {6,15,24}
r2=7 → {7,16,25}
r4=3 → {3,12,21}
r5=5 → {5,14,23}
r6=7 → {7,16,25}
r7=8 → {8,17,26}
r8=9 → {9,18}
r9=1 → {1,10,19}
r10=8 → {8,17,26}

A strictly increasing realisation is forced in the tail:
p4=12, p5=14, p6=16, p7=17, p8=18, p9=19, p10=26
giving dr 3,5,7,8,9,1,8 respectively. Then p1=6, p2=7 and 7<p3<12.

Shape commonality is interpreted by the colleagues as the two-digit ranks being “up-hill’’ i.e. tens<units, which is the natural shape condition on the pre-reduction numbers. With leading zeros this admits p3=08 or 09. 08 gives the consecutive start 06,07,08.

With p3=8:
p = 6 F,7 G,8 H,12 L,14 N,16 P,17 Q,18 R,19 S,26 Z
dr = 6,7,8,3,5,7,8,9,1,8
The block is strictly increasing, all consonants, and the pre-reduction numbers 06,07,08,12,14,16,17,18,19,26 are all tens<units. The missing entry is H with digital root 8, i.e. [H,8].

Alternative completions exist in the same increasing digital-root chain:
p3=9 → I with dr 9 → [I,9];
p3=10 → J with dr 1;
p3=11 → K with dr 2.
These are noted by several colleagues as viable under a looser shape reading. The majority of the deliberation favours the minimal up-hill completion 06-07-08 and the consonant block F G H L N P Q R S Z, giving [H,8]; the I,9 variant is the next admissible non-vowel-breaking choice. Unresolved is whether the shape filter is meant as tens<units or as a straight-line/curved letter filter, which would push toward the I,9 solution advocated by Colleagues 1,13,9.

11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The core constraints are p1< p2< … < p10 in alphabet positions 1-26, ri = digit-sum of pi (with iterative digital root to single digit for cases like 19→10→1), and the written numbers are ri.  
Given ri = 6,7,?,3,5,7,8,9,1,8.

The tail is forced by increasing digital-root possibilities:
r4=3 → {3,12,21}
r5=5 → {5,14,23}
r6=7 → {7,16,25}
r7=8 → {8,17,26}
r8=9 → {9,18}
r9=1 → {1,10,19}
r10=8 → {8,17,26}
The only strictly increasing completion that closes with r9=1,r10=8 is
p4=12 L , p5=14 N , p6=16 P , p7=17 Q , p8=18 R , p9=19 S , p10=26 Z
with digital roots 3,5,7,8,9,1,8 respectively.  
Then p1 must have dr=6 < p2 with dr=7 < p4=12, giving the unique minimal start
p1=6 F , p2=7 G.
Hence 7 < p3 < 12, so p3 ∈ {8,9,10,11} with dr ∈ {8,9,1,2}.

Colleagues 10,11,12,2,6,8 converge on the “up-hill’’ shape reading: the two-digit ranks are required to satisfy tens<units, i.e. the written two-digit form is increasing in shape. With a leading-zero convention that is 06,07,08,12,14,16,17,18,19,26. The natural continuation of 06,07 is 08, giving
p3=8 H, dr=8.
That yields the increasing block F G H L N P Q R S Z with written roots 6,7,8,3,5,7,8,9,1,8, and the ten two-digit ranks are all up-hill.

Minority variants note that p3=9 I with dr=9, p3=10 J with dr=1, p3=11 K with dr=2 are also algebraically admissible in the 7<p3<12 interval. Colleague 1/13/9 argue for a straight-line/“no-curve’’ letter filter leading to I 9; colleague 14 argues for a curved-digit shape filter leading to K 2. Those conflict with the up-hill digit-shape consensus that makes 08 the canonical immediate successor of 06-07.

Unresolved: exact formalisation of Hint 2 “shape’’. The majority interpretation used in the discussion is shape of the two-digit rank, tens<units, which selects the 06-07-08 start and forces p3=8 H. Iterative digital root is required for 19→1. Current best belief is [H,8] as the intended minimal completion, with [I,9] as the next admissible alternative in the same increasing digital-root chain.

